the three ubiquitous downloading status values

Foundation declared NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusNotDownloaded in NSURL.h and defined it
nowhere, so the symbol did not exist. The fourteen NSURLUbiquitousItem*Key constants were all
present; only the VALUES that NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusKey takes were missing, which is
the half an application actually compares against.

A missing constant is the worst kind of gap: dyld stops on the first one an application touches, in
an order nobody controls.

The values are not guessed. CFURLConstants.c already carries the same three strings under their
kCFURL names, so the pair now agrees rather than contradicting itself. Downloaded and Current were
not declared in the header either, and they are added with NotDownloaded because they are one set
and the next application to reach for one should not hit this again.

--- a/NSURL.m
+++ b/NSURL.m
@@ -127,6 +127,11 @@
 NSString *const NSURLTagNamesKey = @"NSURLTagNamesKey";
 NSString *const NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingErrorKey = @"NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingErrorKey";
 NSString *const NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusKey = @"NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusKey";
+// The VALUES that key takes. Only the key existed, so any app comparing against one of these
+// stopped in dyld. Values from CFURLConstants.c lines 95 to 97, which is the same set macOS ships.
+NSString *const NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusNotDownloaded = @"NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusNotDownloaded";
+NSString *const NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusDownloaded = @"NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusDownloaded";
+NSString *const NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusCurrent = @"NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingStatusCurrent";
 NSString *const NSURLApplicationIsScriptableKey = @"NSURLApplicationIsScriptableKey";
 NSString *const NSURLCanonicalPathKey = @"NSURLCanonicalPathKey";
 NSString *const NSURLDocumentIdentifierKey = @"NSURLDocumentIdentifierKey";
